WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.480
Acetylcholine slows the heart.

2
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:09.560
The same acetylcholine makes the muscle around your airways contract.

3
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.720
Who decides the answer, the molecule or the cell?

4
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.920
The molecule carries no instruction about the answer.

5
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.280
Each cell decides, through its receptor, its G protein and the proteins waiting

6
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.280
downstream.

7
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:27.673
This receptor is a G-protein-coupled receptor, or GPCR.

8
00:00:27.673 --> 00:00:31.593
Its chain crosses the membrane seven times, as seven helices.

9
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:36.233
Acetylcholine is an agonist, a ligand that activates its receptor.

10
00:00:36.853 --> 00:00:40.673
It binds in a pocket among the helices and favors the active shape.

11
00:00:41.393 --> 00:00:45.653
In that shape the inner end of helix 6 swings outward and opens a cavity.

12
00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:53.167
Waiting just inside the membrane is a G protein, and its Gα alpha part holds GDP.

13
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:58.147
Gβ and Gγ gamma bind each other so tightly that they act as one unit, βγ.

14
00:00:58.907 --> 00:01:03.367
The active receptor grips the G protein and loosens Gα's hold on GDP.

15
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:06.627
GDP leaves, and GTP takes the empty site.

16
00:01:07.347 --> 00:01:12.587
In the textbook picture, Gα with GTP then lets go of the receptor and of βγ.

17
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:19.620
The cell has two messengers, Gα with GTP and free βγ, and each can find a target.

18
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.200
In pacemaker cells of the heart, the receptor is M2.

19
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.980
It couples to a G protein called Gi.

20
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:31.280
Freed βγ opens a potassium channel, and potassium leaves, moving the voltage away

21
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.160
from threshold. Gα lowers cAMP, cutting pacemaker current.

22
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.460
The heart slows.

23
00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:41.353
In airway muscle, the receptor is M3.

24
00:01:41.993 --> 00:01:44.653
It couples to a different G protein, called Gq.

25
00:01:44.653 --> 00:01:48.533
Gα with GTP switches on an enzyme, phospholipase C-β.

26
00:01:48.533 --> 00:01:51.273
beta. The enzyme cuts PIP2.

27
00:01:51.893 --> 00:01:55.273
IP3 releases stored calcium, and the muscle contracts.

28
00:01:56.367 --> 00:02:00.567
In the heart, acetylcholine meets M2 and Gi, and the heart slows.

29
00:02:01.347 --> 00:02:04.967
In the airways, it meets M3 and Gq, and the muscle contracts.

30
00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:07.267
The acetylcholine is the same.

31
00:02:07.807 --> 00:02:10.747
The receptor and the G protein it couples to are different.

32
00:02:11.247 --> 00:02:16.607
So keep this rule: receptor, G protein and downstream proteins decide, not the

33
00:02:16.607 --> 00:02:16.847
signal.
